  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 330 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThe highlight of the long term forecast period will be a slow \ntransition back out of the hot and dry weather pattern back into \nmore seasonal temperatures and occasional rainfall chances. Another \nweekend heat wave is on tap for Saturday and Sunday. For the fifth \nconsecutive weekend, a broad upper level ridge aloft will support \nabove average temperatures across our area. 700-mb temperatures \naround +16C to +20C are not quite as extreme as the last two weekend \nheatwaves, but still sufficiently high for continued near record \nheat. Expect highs both days in the lower 90s for Laramie, Rawlins, \nand Saratoga, and then mid 90s to lower 100s over the High Plains. \nCurrently, record highs are only forecast at Laramie and Cheyenne, \nbut all locations will be within 1 to 3 degrees of daily record \nhighs. This heat wave does not look as likely to challenge all-time \nor monthly records, but we will certainly be hot. The chance for \nstorms looks very limited on Saturday, but we will finally start to \nsee precipitable water return to near average on Sunday. The upper \nlevel ridge will start to weaken and slowly nudge eastward, allowing \nmonsoon moisture to fill to the north once again. We may see a few \nshowers or thunderstorms creeping into our southern mountains by \nSunday, but this will probably still be quite limited. While the \nprobability for rainfall will be low, forecast soundings favorable \nfor dry microbursts, so gusty winds may be the initial concern as \nmoisture tries to return to the area.\n\nEnsembles show precipitable water continuing to gradually improve \nthrough the week ahead as monsoon moisture expands to the north once \nagain. Coverage will probably be fairly limited early in the week, \nbut look for higher chances for precipitation towards the middle to \nlater part of the work week. While we are still a ways out for \ndetails, this doesn't really look like a pure monsoon pattern. A few \nintrusions of shortwave troughs to our north may keep vertical wind \nshear high enough to get a few strong to severe thunderstorms. As \nmoisture mixes through the boundary layer, we should also ease the \nfire weather concerns as humidity climbs.",
